/-!
# Order of jmath̄ at its zeros divides three, under a branch bound

Theorem statement: `ModularCurve.ord_jBar_dvd_three_of_pos_of_forall_isRoot_hasRamBound`
(`dad57c49-c8dc-5825-8dbc-8d3f730c3378`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `bc8c131f-e3f0-5881-a2f4-af2e3d95c365`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $N$ be a nonzero natural number and let `data` be a modular polynomial datum of level $N$: a
polynomial $\Phi \in \mathbb{Z}[x][y]$ which is monic in $y$, of degree in $y$ equal to $\sum_{d
\mid N,\ d \text{ squarefree}} N/d$, and which satisfies $\Phi(\jmath(q), \jmath(q^N)) = 0$, where
$\jmath(q) = q^{-1}\cdot(1 + \cdots)$ is the Laurent series `jq` over $\mathbb{Q}$ and the
substitution of $\jmath(q)$ into integer polynomials is `evalAtJ`. Assume the hypothesis `htrio`:
for every Hahn series $r$ with rational exponents and coefficients in $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ which
is a root of the one-variable polynomial obtained from $\Phi$ by casting integer coefficients into
$\mathrm{HahnSeries}\,\mathbb{Q}\,\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ and evaluating the first variable at the
monomial $q$ (the Hahn series `single 1 1`), the support of $r$ is contained in $\{k/3 : k \in
\mathbb{Z}\}$. Let $F$ be the $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-subfield of
$\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ generated by the coefficientwise images of the
$\mathbb{Q}$-subfield of $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}\,\mathbb{Q}$ generated by the expansions
$\jmath(q^d)$ for the nonzero divisors $d$ of $N$, and let $v$ be a place of $F$ over
$\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, that is, a valuation subring of $F$ containing $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$,
distinct from $F$ and a principal ideal ring. Write $\bar\jmath \in F$ for the image of $\jmath(q)$
under the coefficient embedding. If $v.\mathrm{ord}(\bar\jmath) > 0$, then
$v.\mathrm{ord}(\bar\jmath)$ divides $3$ in $\mathbb{Z}$, where $\mathrm{ord}$ is minus the
logarithm of the associated adic valuation.

This is the local shape of the covering of the $j$-line by the level $N$ modular curve above the
elliptic point of order three: the positivity hypothesis selects the places at which $\bar\jmath$
vanishes, and the assumed denominator bound on the Newton–Puiseux branches of $\Phi(q,y) = 0$ bounds
the ramification indices there. It is the conditional form of
[`ModularCurve.ord_jBar_dvd_three`](thm.html#ModularCurve.ord_jBar_dvd_three), which cites it.

